Here's a simple function in JavaScript that accomplishes this task. The function checks if a number is prime by checking divisibility only up to the square root of the number (as a prime number must be greater than 1 and have no divisors other than 1 and itself).

```javascript
function isPrime(num) {
    if (num <= 1) {
        return false;
    }
    if (num <= 3) {
        return true;
    }
    if (num % 2 === 0 || num % 3 === 0) {
        return false;
    }
    let sqrtNum = Math.sqrt(num);
    for (let i = 5; i <= sqrtNum; i += 6) {
        if (num % i === 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}

function checkAndPrintPrimes(arr) {
    for (let number of arr) {
        if (isPrime(number)) {
            console.log(number);
        }
    }
}

// Example usage:
let numbers = [2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23, 29];
checkAndPrintPrimes(numbers);
```

This script defines two functions:

1. `isPrime()`: This function checks if a given number is prime. It uses a optimization technique by only checking divisibility up to the square root of the number, reducing the number of checks significantly.
2. `checkAndPrintPrimes()`: This function iterates through the input array and calls the `isPrime()` function on each element. If the element is prime, it is printed to the console.

The example usage demonstrates how to use the `checkAndPrintPrimes()` function with an array of numbers.

Below is a basic example of how you might create a modal with a chatbot using HTML and JavaScript. This example includes four messages, with two messages from the AI chatbot and two from the human user.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Chatbot Modal</title>
    <style>
       .chat-container {
            display: inline-block;
            margin: 10px auto;
            width: 200px;
            background-color: #f2f2f2;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 5px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
        }

       .bot-message {
            border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
            font-size: 20px;
            padding: 10px;
            margin-top: 10px;
        }

       .user-message {
            border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
            font-size: 18px;
            padding: 10px;
            margin-top: 10px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="chat-container">
    <h3>AI Chatbot</h3>
    <p>Previously typed messages from AI:</p>
    <div class="bot-message">
        <p>AI Response 1:</p>
        <span>Hi there, how can I assist you today?</span>
    </div>

    <p>Previously typed messages from human:</p>
    <div class="user-message">
        <p>From human:</p>
        <span>Hi there, how can I assist you today?</span>
    </div>

    <p>AI Response 2:</p>
    <span>Great! Let's chat.</span>
</div>

<script>
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
        const botMessages = ['AI Response 1', 'AI Response 2'];
        const humanMessages = ['From human', 'Hi there, how can I assist you today?'];

        let messages = [];

        // Add messages from AI and human to the messages array
        for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            messages.push(`AI says: ${botMessages[i]}`);
        }

        for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            messages.push(`Human says: ${humanMessages[i]}`);
        }

        // Create modal and append messages
        const modal = document.createElement('div');
        modal.className = 'chat-container';
        modal.innerHTML = `
            <div>
                <h3>Chatbot</h3>
                <p>Previously typed messages from AI:</p>
                <div class="bot-message">
                    <p>AI says: ${botMessages[i]}</p>
                </div>
                <p>Previously typed messages from human:</p>
                <div class="user-message">
                    <p>From human:</p>
                    <span>Hi there, how can I assist you today?</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        `;
        document.body.appendChild(modal);

        // Show the modal when the page loads
        window.onclick = event => modal.style.display = event.target.matches? 'none' : 'block';
    });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

This code creates a simple modal where the AI chatbot and human user messages are displayed. When the page loads, the modal is shown, and when the user clicks inside it, the messages are hidden or displayed as per the user's input. The messages are pre-loaded into an array for efficiency.

In TypeScript, you can leverage the type annotations and assertions to communicate with the type system about the expected types of variables, functions, and expressions. While TypeScript doesn't natively support the concept of knowing in a practical sense about a value's actual type for all possible scenarios (e.g., having multiple types in practice but knowing it will only ever be one), you can use type assertions and type declarations to guide the type checker in making assumptions that align with your intentions.

Here's how you can approach this:

### Using Type Assertions

Type assertions allow you to assert a specific type within a variable or function declaration. While these assertions are not "truthful" in the sense that they enforce the constraint in all possible scenarios, they can be very helpful in guiding the compiler or other tools (like linters or formatters) about your expectations.

**Example:** 

```typescript
// Assuming you know the type of a variable will only ever be `Number` in practice.
let myVariable: number = 42;

// Or if you're sure it will only be `String` and you want to enforce this.
let myStringVariable: string = "Hello, world!";
```

### Using Type Guards

Type guards allow you to create conditional type definitions based on certain conditions. They can be used to declare a default or expected type when there's no explicit type hint.

**Example:** 

```typescript
// A function that might handle an object with different properties.
function handleObject(obj: any): void {
    if (typeof obj === 'object') {
        // Type assertion for practical use case
        if (obj instanceof Object) {
            // Type guard for common object properties
            if (Object.keys(obj).length > 0) {
                handleObject(obj[key]);
            }
        } else {
            // Handle non-object values or specific types here
            console.log('The value is not an object.');
        }
    } else {
        // Handle other cases here
        console.log('The value is not an object.');
    }
}

// Usage
handleObject({ name: 'Alice' });
handleObject({ age: 30 });
handleObject(42); // This will log 'The value is not an object.'
```

### Using Conditional Type Definitions

Sometimes, you might want to conditionally define a type based on certain conditions. This approach can help guide the compiler in understanding your intent.

**Example:**

```typescript
// Conditional type for a function argument that might take a string or an array of strings
function processStrings(array: string[]): void {
    if (array.length > 0) {
        // Process each string in the array
        array.forEach(string => {
            // Do something with the string
        });
    } else {
        // Do nothing or log a message if the array is empty
        console.log('No strings to process.');
    }
}

// Usage
processStrings(['Hello', 'World', 'This is a test']);
```

### Conclusion

While TypeScript doesn't inherently support the concept of knowing a variable's type across all possible scenarios in a practical sense, these approaches can help guide the compiler or other tools in understanding your intentions. Through judicious use of type assertions, type guards, and conditional type definitions, you can effectively communicate your expectations to the type system.

